    "arObjKomputerDetailDef":  "A computer is an end device that users use to send and receive data over a network.",
    "arObjKomputerDetailFunction":  "In a network, the computer is a source or destination of data: it creates requests, sends data to other devices, and receives data from them.",
    "arObjKomputerDetailExtra":  "Examples of computer activity in a network: opening web pages, sending email, uploading files, and streaming video. All of these are essentially processes of sending and receiving data.",
    "arObjSwitchDetailDef":  "A network device that connects many devices within a single local area network (LAN).",
    "arObjSwitchDetailFunction":  "The switch connects devices in a local network and forwards data only to the destination device, so network traffic stays more efficient than with a hub.",
    "arObjSwitchDetailExtra":  "How it works: the switch reads the destination MAC address on each frame and forwards it to the port connected to the destination device using its MAC table.",
    "arObjHubDetailDef":  "A simple network device that connects several devices within one network.",
    "arObjHubDetailFunction":  "The hub connects devices and forwards incoming data to every connected port, without knowing which device is the destination.",
    "arObjHubDetailExtra":  "Difference from a switch: the hub broadcasts data to all devices, while the switch forwards data only to the destination device based on the MAC address. Hubs are therefore less efficient and more prone to collisions.",
    "arObjRouterDetailDef":  "A network device that connects two or more different networks.",
    "arObjRouterDetailFunction":  "The router forwards data packets between networks, choosing the best path toward the destination network.",
    "arObjRouterDetailExtra":  "How it works: the router reads the destination IP address on the packet and decides the forwarding path using its routing table. Routers let devices from different networks communicate, for example between a home LAN and the internet.",
    "arObjTopologiDetailDef":  "A network layout in which every device connects directly to a single central device, usually a switch or hub.",
    "arObjTopologiDetailFunction":  "The central device acts as the shared connection point: all data between devices passes through it before being forwarded to its destination.",
    "arObjTopologiDetailExtra":  "Advantages: easy to add devices and easy to detect a faulty cable. Limitation: if the central device fails, the whole network stops working (single point of failure).",
